namespace TiaMcp.Host;

internal static class AuthenticationInstructions
{
    internal const string Text = """
        When the selected project or CPU requires user authentication, prompt the user for both the local username and password, identifying the exact project/CPU. Reuse credentials already provided for that target in this session instead of asking again. Do not assume credentials for one CPU apply to other CPUs or adapter-generated scratch projects.
        WarningPasswordForProtectionError and SimulationConfigurationPasswordRequired concern the separate confidential PLC configuration-protection password, not a project/user login or a license failure. Ask for that protection password for the identified target; request username and password separately only if user authentication is also required. Use an already supplied, validated generated credential reference where supported.
        Current tools do not accept raw login credentials. simulation_provision_scratch_credential can attempt runtime protection-password provisioning only for a ticket-bound owned local PLCSIM instance using its already validated generated DPAPI credential reference. plc_online_compare, plc_station_upload and the db_snapshot change kinds log in to such an instance only with runtimeCredentialMetadataPath, the generated runtime-user credential reference that simulation_download loaded onto it. For other targets, direct credential entry to the TIA authentication UI until a supported secure input flow exists; do not invent tool parameters. Never put passwords in source, client configuration, documentation, reports or logs, and never retry guessed/default passwords or disable protection to bypass authentication.
        For runtime_web_* and runtime_ua_* username authentication, use their public credential-binding tool and the supported local protected-credential CLI. Supply only the resulting protected reference to the connection tool; enter the secret through the CLI's hidden prompt or redirected standard input. OPC UA client_identity returns only the public application certificate for explicit server-side trust provisioning. A certificate pin or matching application URI does not prove the running PLC program.
        A TLS certificate verification callback is separate from password authentication. The generated-scratch provisioner reports public certificate metadata and rejects trust by default. Enable trustOwnedEmptyCpuCertificate only after explicit user authorization for first-use trust of certificates from fresh, verified, owned empty local simulator instances. Reuse that authorization within its stated scope. Each attempt pins its first certificate and rejects subsequent changes; this is user-authorized first-use trust, not independent certificate authentication. plc_station_upload has the same opt-in as trustOwnedSimulationCertificate for its ticket-bound owned simulator instance only; enable it only after explicit user authorization, and its pin is likewise limited to one upload attempt. When the upload's TLS challenge offers no certificate, the same opt-in accepts it as unauthenticated TLS for that owned instance only; its identity rests on the ownership checks, not a certificate. Never infer either authorization from a request to run tests or from the certificate subject or network address alone.
        """;
}
